Keep a burst of incoming UDP instead of dropping it, for programs that embed xray.

core.DialUDP hands out a PacketConn whose incoming packets wait in a channel
of 16 (transport/internet/udp/dispatcher.go, DialDispatcher). When it is full,
callback() releases the packet without a word. keqrnel gives every UDP
session from sing-box's TUN and SOCKS inbounds to xray through this
PacketConn (bridge/xray, ListenPacket), and sing-box reads it one packet at a
time while writing each into the TUN, so any burst longer than 16 that
arrives faster than that is cut.

That is the shape of a video frame in a call or a screen share, and of a game
server's snapshot. Measured on an isolated netns lab with keqdroid's Linux
keqrnel, VLESS REALITY Vision, 2026-10-14: 30 fps x 40 packets of 1200 bytes
from the server lost 30-39% on every TUN stack, MTU and EIN setting; 64
packets back to back lost 13-38%. Upload lost nothing, and mihomo on the same
lab lost nothing. A queue of 128 already brought every test to 0%; 512 leaves
room for larger frames. A build with 16 left as is reproduced the loss, so the
queue is the whole cause.

Each waiting packet holds one pooled buffer, so a session costs memory only
while its reader is behind.

Applied by tools/patchxray. keqdroid builds its Android libxray.so from the
same tree; standalone xray does not use core.DialUDP, so nothing changes there.

diff --git a/transport/internet/udp/dispatcher.go b/transport/internet/udp/dispatcher.go
index 963ce66..73fdfd1 100644
--- a/transport/internet/udp/dispatcher.go
+++ b/transport/internet/udp/dispatcher.go
@@ -171,7 +171,9 @@ type dispatcherConn struct {
 
 func DialDispatcher(ctx context.Context, dispatcher routing.Dispatcher) (net.PacketConn, error) {
 	c := &dispatcherConn{
-		cache: make(chan *udp.Packet, 16),
+		// keqrnel: 16 dropped a burst whenever the reader lagged, see
+		// patches/xray-udp-dial-queue.patch in keqrnel
+		cache: make(chan *udp.Packet, 512),
 		done:  done.New(),
 		ctx:   ctx,
 	}
